\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Our primary contribution is methodological: layer selection is a critical, underexamined researcher degree of freedom in the probing literature. A biased composite metric incorporating crossing rate systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, where representation compression inflates threshold crossings independently of discriminative power, masking the HS advantage entirely. This finding generalizes beyond safety probing to any study that evaluates threshold-dependent metrics across transformer layers. The five-step audit protocol we propose (\cref{sec:discussion}) requires negligible additional compute and would have prevented the spurious scale-dependent conclusion in our initial analysis.

The empirical contribution is conditional on both encoder quality and model-specific factors. HS probes show a robust advantage at 7--8B (strong evidence for OT-7B, moderate for R1-8B) and a suggestive but position-mediated advantage at 32B (moderate for QwQ-32B, qualified for R1-32B with residualized gap NS). The advantage narrows from 4/4 models HB-significant against 384d MiniLM to 1/4 against 1024d BGE-large, establishing an encoder-quality boundary rather than an absolute modality difference. Current probes characterize the commitment phenomenon (FPR 39--52\%, oracle gap 85.5\% on R1-8B) rather than constitute production detectors; causal interventions~\citep{meng2022rome,li2024iti} and LLM-based text encoders~\citep{wang2024e5mistral} are the primary next steps toward closing this gap.

