\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Both temporal context and modality choice improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}); at 32B, relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent improvement regardless of modality, while modality choice requires principled layer selection.

\paragraph{Encoder-quality boundary as a finding.}
The HS advantage is a function of text encoder quality: significant against 384d MiniLM across all evidence tiers (4/4 models$^\dagger$ HB) but attenuated against 1024d BGE-large (1/4 HB). At matched 384d via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists (+16.8 to +23.1pp, HB corrected), providing partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. This encoder-quality boundary is itself a finding: the HS advantage represents information not yet captured by current sentence encoders rather than a fundamental modality difference. LLM-based text encoders (e.g., E5-Mistral-7B, 4096d; \citealp{wang2024e5mistral}) would likely further narrow the gap, bounding the practical lifetime of any HS advantage claim.

